WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.000
In this lesson we'll go through keeping a Scratchpad healthy. Writing one is the easy part.

2
00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:10.000
Maintaining it over days and weeks is where most projects fall short.

3
00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:17.000
We'll cover length discipline, structure, pruning policy, how to handle conflicts with Cloud.md,

4
00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:24.000
multi-developer coordination, and privacy boundaries. It's a lot to go over, so let's get into it.

5
00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:28.000
A Scratchpad earns its keep only while it stays accurate.

6
00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:34.000
Scratchpad hygiene is the discipline of keeping the file accurate, pruned, and consistently structured.

7
00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:40.000
It isn't a one-time setup task. It is an ongoing practice that has to happen every session.

8
00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:48.000
Then there is the boundary between Cloud.md and the Scratchpad, which is the most important conflict to understand.

9
00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:56.000
Cloud.md holds stable rules that apply across all sessions, while the Scratchpad holds evolving session state.

10
00:00:56.000 --> 00:01:03.000
When they say different things, Cloud.md wins, and that priority needs to be explicit in how you configure the agent.

11
00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:07.000
Pruning policy is the defined rule for what gets removed and when.

12
00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:12.802
Without a policy, nothing ever leaves the file, and it grows into a record of every thought anyone

13
00:01:12.802 --> 00:01:15.940
ever had about the project, including the wrong ones.

14
00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:21.000
Scratchpads bloat fast. Every session adds entries.

15
00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:26.000
Sessions that resolve items rarely take the time to remove the old ones.

16
00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:32.000
Over a week of active work, a Scratchpad that started at 50 lines can easily reach 500.

17
00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:37.000
At that length, it is slower for the agent to process and harder to reason over.

18
00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:43.364
The fix is a cap with a pruning trigger. Pick a threshold and treat crossing it as a signal to do a

19
00:01:43.364 --> 00:01:45.935
pruning pass before adding more content.

20
00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:51.863
Remove entries that are fully resolved. Archive completed task state to a separate history file if

21
00:01:51.863 --> 00:01:52.940
you need a record.

22
00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:57.000
Rewrite the current state block rather than appending to it.

23
00:01:57.000 --> 00:02:04.000
These habits together keep the file at a useful size and prevent it from becoming an obstacle instead of an aid.

24
00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:09.000
Structure matters because it makes the file scannable for both you and the agent.

25
00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:15.413
I recommend four blocks. First, dated entries at the top, with a timestamp on each so you can

26
00:02:15.413 --> 00:02:18.931
identify stale notes quickly during a pruning pass.

27
00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:24.937
Second, a decision log for architectural choices and their rationale. This block changes rarely

28
00:02:24.937 --> 00:02:27.937
because good decisions have a long, useful life.

29
00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:33.000
Third, a current state block that gets rewritten each session rather than appended to.

30
00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:38.000
This is the block that tells the next session exactly where work stands right now.

31
00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:44.000
Fourth, an open questions block for unresolved issues the next session needs to address.

32
00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:47.000
When a question is answered, the entry comes out.

33
00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:54.000
These four blocks give the Scratchpad a shape that stays manageable even under active, sustained use.

34
00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:59.000
Let's be precise about the boundary between clod.md and the Scratchpad.

35
00:02:59.000 --> 00:03:06.155
Clod.md holds stable rules. Coding conventions, tool permissions, standing instructions about how

36
00:03:06.155 --> 00:03:07.926
the agent should behave.

37
00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:12.000
These change rarely and represent the settled state of the project.

38
00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:15.000
The Scratchpad holds evolving session state.

39
00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:20.000
What is in progress? What decisions were made this session? What questions are open?

40
00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:22.000
These change every session.

41
00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:26.000
When the two contradict each other, clod.md takes precedence.

42
00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:30.000
This priority needs to be explicit in the clod.md itself.

43
00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:37.000
Something like, the Scratchpad records session state, and in case of conflict, these rules apply.

44
00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:43.000
Without that explicit hierarchy, the agent may try to reconcile the two in unpredictable ways.

45
00:03:43.000 --> 00:03:49.000
Make the precedence rule visible and unambiguous so there is no room for misinterpretation.

46
00:03:49.000 --> 00:03:55.000
When a Scratchpad is committed to Git, it becomes a shared resource across the team.

47
00:03:55.000 --> 00:03:58.000
That is mostly a good thing.

48
00:03:58.000 --> 00:04:03.000
Developers can read each other's session state, see decisions that were made without them,

49
00:04:03.000 --> 00:04:07.000
and avoid re-exploring dead ends their colleagues already documented.

50
00:04:07.000 --> 00:04:11.000
But shared Scratchpads introduce coordination overhead.

51
00:04:11.000 --> 00:04:16.932
Merge conflicts on the Scratchpad signal that two people worked on the same task concurrently, which

52
00:04:16.932 --> 00:04:17.940
is worth knowing.

53
00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:24.000
The current state block needs clear ownership when two developers are working in parallel.

54
00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:29.000
A simple convention is that whoever is driving the active task owns that block.

55
00:04:29.000 --> 00:04:35.000
For small teams with clear task ownership, one Scratchpad per repo works well.

56
00:04:35.000 --> 00:04:43.000
Larger teams with parallel workstreams may want per-developer or per-feature Scratchpad files instead.

57
00:04:43.000 --> 00:04:46.000
Privacy is an active choice, not a default.

58
00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:50.000
Before you commit the Scratchpad, review what it actually contains.

59
00:04:50.000 --> 00:04:55.748
This sounds obvious, but under deadline pressure, people paste things into the Scratchpad quickly

60
00:04:55.748 --> 00:04:57.940
without thinking about commit safety.

61
00:04:58.000 --> 00:05:03.000
Credentials, tokens, and API keys should never appear in a committed file.

62
00:05:03.000 --> 00:05:08.000
If the nature of your work means the Scratchpad frequently contains sensitive context,

63
00:05:08.000 --> 00:05:12.000
add it to .gitignore and treat it as a local-only file.

64
00:05:12.000 --> 00:05:19.000
Document that choice explicitly in clod.md so future developers know the Scratchpad isn't tracked.

65
00:05:19.000 --> 00:05:24.000
The tradeoff is that a git-ignored Scratchpad can't be shared via git.

66
00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:26.000
You lose the team coordination benefit.

67
00:05:26.000 --> 00:05:34.000
Make that tradeoff consciously and explicitly rather than discovering it after a credential ends up in a commit.

68
00:05:34.000 --> 00:05:39.000
The graveyard failure mode is the most common way Scratchpads go wrong.

69
00:05:39.000 --> 00:05:42.000
It happens when entries accumulate without removal.

70
00:05:42.000 --> 00:05:48.360
The file grows into a record of everything that was ever true about the project, including things

71
00:05:48.360 --> 00:05:49.934
that are no longer true.

72
00:05:50.000 --> 00:05:54.000
The agent reads the file and treats all of it as current.

73
00:05:54.000 --> 00:05:56.000
Stale notes propagate into decisions.

74
00:05:56.000 --> 00:06:04.000
The agent recommends an approach you already ruled out or references a naming convention you changed weeks ago.

75
00:06:04.000 --> 00:06:06.000
The agent isn't being careless.

76
00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:08.000
It is doing exactly what it should.

77
00:06:08.000 --> 00:06:11.000
Trusting the authoritative state file, you gave it.

78
00:06:11.000 --> 00:06:15.000
The problem is that the file stopped being authoritative.

79
00:06:15.000 --> 00:06:19.000
Avoiding the graveyard requires consistent pruning discipline.

80
00:06:19.000 --> 00:06:23.000
Without it, the Scratchpad eventually does more harm than good.

81
00:06:23.000 --> 00:06:28.000
A healthy Scratchpad requires active maintenance, not just initial setup.

82
00:06:28.000 --> 00:06:34.508
Cap the length, prune resolved entries every session, and rewrite the current state block rather

83
00:06:34.508 --> 00:06:35.932
than appending to it.

84
00:06:36.000 --> 00:06:40.000
Keep the structure consistent so the file stays scannable for Claude.

85
00:06:40.000 --> 00:06:48.000
Keep credentials out of anything that you commit, and let Claude.md win whenever the file and the rules disagree.

86
00:06:48.000 --> 00:06:52.000
That's it for this lesson. I'll see you in the next one.

